A||N||9||Bob Englert||Tips on replacing the battery tray?||||1||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||12-27-2000||07:44 PM||||The battery tray in my daugher's 87 coupe is about to fall out of the car I think.  The tray is about 1/4 rusted away and is rattling around often.  I think one of the mounting locations has rusted free.  <P>Can anyone provide suggestions on how to remove the old tray and replace it.  I have a new tray from GM.  Was wondering the best way to cut out the old one.  I would pop rivet the new one in.<P>Thanks again.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000001||PJ||12-27-2000||10:10 PM||||Cut as much as you can of the old one away and grind down the remainder to the last bend. I use a Bosch electric die grinder with cut-off discs and grinder bits. Don't try to remove the original welds as you'll grind through the sheet metal in the wheel wells and the firewall. Ospho the rusty areas, reprime, repaint and reinstall. Repeat in about ten or fifteen years. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GeeDee86GT||12-28-2000||11:01 AM||||Bob:<BR>Rivet the new tray rather than trying to weld it in.  If the tray is rusty maybe that side panel is a s well? The lastest Street Rod Builder mag has a how-to on a new product from Dow Automotive.  It is for reinforcing metal and is a epoxy impregnated 'glass patch.  Fix the rust out by riveting easy-to- mold thin sheet metal and apply this patch over the whole thing.  It is heat activated (heat gun) and should harden to equal the original panel.  Looks like great stuff.  Called  Beta-Brace 85001 Reinforcing Composite.<BR>Gary||||reg||7
Z||000003||fropuff||12-28-2000||11:40 AM||||Or, you can buy a kit from Archie? and put the battery in the front.  Most people that do it want to get close to a 50/50 weight balance on their cars, so they put it in the front.  You need to get new cables, but I think that's all.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||12-28-2000||06:06 PM||||I did mine pretty much as PJ has described.  The flanges from the old tray remain spot welded against the firewall and wheel well.  The new tray is simply riveted right over the old flanges.  Use big rivets, at least 3/16" dia.||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||01-02-2001||10:00 PM||||Is the battery tray a dealer replacement item or must you find one in a bone yard?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||01-03-2001||07:30 AM||||They are still available new from GM.  It is a 2 part assembly and you should order both pieces when you replace it.  <P>I can try to find the P/N's if you need them.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||01-03-2001||07:49 AM||||Yes -- Please post the part numbers for the battery tray parts.<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000008||HarryG||01-03-2001||11:56 AM||||While you're replacing the tray, you might want to check the fuel lines running underneath. Shortly after I replaced my tray, I had a fuel line spring a leak; I think it was from battery acid corrosion. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||01-03-2001||05:30 PM||||According to my 22P book:<P>Batery Tray - 10071787<BR>Hold Down Support - 20489129<P>Both were still available from GM the last time I ordered them.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
